\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


Subgroup membership asks whether an input matrix is a finite word in the two displayed generators and their inverses. The matrices have determinant one, so their products lie in $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb Q)$.

The group $\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb Z)$ consists of integer matrices with integer inverses, and $\mathrm{SL}_n$ imposes determinant one. The subgroup generated by matrices consists of finite products of them and their inverses. A centralizer consists of elements commuting with the specified element.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For an integer $K\geq2$, let $H(K)\leq\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb Q)$ be generated by

$$A(K)=\begin{pmatrix}1&K\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad B(K)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\K&1\end{pmatrix}.$$

Is there a sublinear-time algorithm for the subgroup membership problem in $H(K)$? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2016/37}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The input length, access model, and meaning of sublinear are not specified; reading an explicitly encoded matrix is a separate cost.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Decide membership in the explicit two-generator matrix group in sublinear time.

