\section{Literature and context check}
The original problem-session passage in OWR 37/2016, p.~2163, was read in the cached primary report. It asks about integer $K\ge2$ but supplies no encoding or cost convention. The abstract of Chorna--Geller--Shpilrain, arXiv:1605.05226v4, was also consulted; it concerns membership and its complexity, but its full theorems were not inspected here. The following elementary argument is a restricted access-model result, not a claimed new answer to that paper or to all interpretations of sublinear time.
\section{Research attempt: A bit-query obstruction on upper unipotent inputs}

\paragraph{Exact intersection calculation.}
Fix an integer $K\ge2$, and put $U(M)=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&M\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ for an integer $M$. Then
\[
 U(M)\in H(K)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad K\mid M.
\]
The reverse implication is $U(M)=A(K)^{M/K}$. For the forward direction, combine adjacent powers of the same generator and remove powers with exponent zero in any representing word. Apply the resulting alternating word to $e_1=(1,0)^T$, from right to left. A rightmost power of $A$ fixes $e_1$ and can be discarded for this calculation. If any $B$ remains, its first application gives $(1,bK)^T$, with $b\ne0$, so $|y|>|x|$ and $y\ne0$. Whenever $|y|>|x|$, a nonzero power $A^a$ gives
\[
 |x+aKy|\ge K|y|-|x|>|y|.
\]
Whenever $|x|>|y|$, a nonzero power $B^b$ similarly gives $|y+bKx|>|x|$. Thus after the first $B$, the second coordinate never vanishes: an $A$ step preserves it and a $B$ step makes its absolute value strictly positive. A word containing $B$ therefore cannot be upper triangular. A word without $B$ is $A^a$, proving the assertion without invoking a freeness theorem.

\paragraph{Linear input-bit lower bound.}
Take the fixed value $K=3$, or any fixed odd $K\ge3$. The input is an explicitly encoded integer matrix, with random access to individual binary digits; exact membership must be correct on every valid input. For $L\ge1$, let
\[
 M_0=K2^L,\qquad M_i=K2^L+2^i\quad(0\le i<L).
\]
All matrices $U(M_i)$ have determinant one and the same bit length. Their encodings differ from $U(M_0)$ in exactly one of its bottom $L$ digits. The base matrix belongs to $H(K)$, and none of the alternatives does, since odd $K>1$ cannot divide $2^i$.

On the base input a deterministic exact algorithm must inspect all these $L$ digits. If it omits digit $i$, its entire adaptive transcript is identical on the corresponding alternative, yet the required answers differ. The total input length is $\Theta(L)$, so even this promised subclass precludes a worst-case sublinear bit-query bound.

The conclusion also holds for bounded-error randomization with a worst-case cap of $q$ bit queries. Give the base input probability $1/2$ and distribute the other probability $1/2$ uniformly over the $L$ alternatives. Couple the algorithm's random coins on the base and a random alternative. Until the changed digit is inspected, the two executions coincide. On the base path at most $q$ of the $L$ positions are inspected; consequently the probability of seeing the change is at most $q/L$. The resulting transcript distributions have total variation at most $q/L$: the probability of a differing coupled transcript bounds the difference of probabilities of every event. Under equal priors, any decision rule therefore has average error at least $(1-q/L)/2$, since its acceptance probabilities differ by at most $q/L$. Worst-case error at most $1/3$ forces $q\ge L/3$.

\paragraph{Why this does not settle the unqualified question.}
The obstruction charges access to digits, not arithmetic operations on an already loaded integer, and concerns worst-case exact or bounded-error correctness, not generic-case rejection. It does not rule out a sublinear bound in the numerical magnitude $\max |M_{ij}|$, a promise excluding these inputs, or a word-RAM convention making long-integer operations free. For $K=2$ this particular upper-unipotent restriction merely tests parity and has a one-bit algorithm; extending the obstruction to every $K$ would require a different family. The source supplies none of the conventions needed to identify these variants.
\section{Verification and adversarial audit}
The alternating-word argument was checked for the empty word, a pure $A$ power, negative exponents, $K=2$, and a word beginning or ending with either generator. Strict dominance, rather than positive entries, is what handles cancellation. All adversarial inputs are integral determinant-one matrices with equal encoding lengths. The randomized argument uses a distribution on valid matrices and explicitly states its worst-case query cap; it is not an unexplained appeal to an information bound.
\paragraph{Audit corrections.} Rejected an unproved congruence-only characterization of the entire subgroup. The proof instead establishes only the upper-unipotent intersection and keeps the $K=2$ boundary and encoding dependence explicit.
\paragraph{External computation.} None; symbolic arguments and boundary checks only.
\section{Scope and remaining gaps}
The proved lower bound is complete for the stated bit-query promise subclass with fixed odd $K\ge3$. The original access model, cost parameter, all-$K$ interpretation, and possible generic-case algorithms remain unspecified or untreated. No full-source resolution or novelty claim is made.
